{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the birth of facebook and the complex web of relationships, ambition, and betrayal that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a sharp, fast-paced drama that examines the cost of innovation in the digital age.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached. his portrayal is chillingly accurate, capturing the quiet intensity and cold calculation of a young man obsessed with building something monumental, even if it means burning bridges. aaron taylor-clarkson, as the co-founder justin o’neal, and jude law, as the lawyer representing the winklevoss twins, both bring nuance and depth to their roles, making the courtroom scenes feel like high-stakes chess matches.\n\nthe screenplay is a marvel—witty, dense, and rhythmically electric. sorkin’s signature rapid-fire dialogue is perfectly suited to the world of tech startups and legal battles, turning what could have been dry exposition into thrilling, emotionally charged exchanges. the pacing never falters; every scene serves a purpose, whether it’s revealing character, advancing the plot, or underscoring the film’s central themes of isolation and ambition.\n\njames newton howard’s score, with its haunting, pulsating electronic tones, complements the film’s tone perfectly. it feels both futuristic and melancholic, mirroring the paradox of connection in an age of digital isolation. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is clean and precise, using cool blues and grays to evoke the sterile, high-tech environments of harvard dorm rooms and silicon valley offices.\n\nwhat makes the social network truly remarkable is how it transcends its subject matter. yes, it’s about facebook, but more importantly, it’s about human connection—or the lack thereof. it asks whether success achieved at the cost of trust and friendship is worth anything at all. the final scene, where mark refreshes his facebook page, searching for a connection he can never quite reach, is devastating in its simplicity.\n\nthe film also handles its legal conflicts with remarkable balance, avoiding easy villains. the winklevoss twins are portrayed not as cartoonish antagonists, but as legitimate claimants to an idea they believed was theirs. even eric sadler, played by brian cox, is given weight and dignity, representing the old guard of privilege that zuckerberg both defies and inherits.\n\nin every technical and narrative aspect, the social network is flawless. it won three oscars, including best adapted screenplay, and deserved every one. it’s a film that doesn’t just entertain—it provokes thought about the nature of modern success, the loneliness of genius, and the price of building empires on the backs of broken relationships. ten years later, it still feels startlingly relevant, a chillingly accurate portrait of how the digital world reshaped not just technology, but humanity itself.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -0.9510456919670105}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.007622679229825735}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token":